import contextlib
import difflib
import fcntl
import functools
import hashlib
import json
import os
import re
import select
import shlex
import shutil
import signal
import statistics
import subprocess
import sys
import tempfile
import threading
import time
import types

VERSION = "{{OK_PLANNER_VERSION}}"
RESULT_LINE_BYTES = 512

USAGE = """tests — the mechanical steps of a sprint's tests: the offers, the
scenario list, the runs, the plan, the tasks a sprint's test stages
file, and the stamp.

  tests offers [--json]
      The groups of test work /plan-sprint offers at Frame, each with
      its key, of the kinds testing.offers lists: stale stories, each
      story whose file's blob id differs from its test stamp, and each
      story with recorded scenarios and no stamp; untested stories, each
      story with no recorded scenario, no test stamp, and no unguarded
      answer naming it; legacy folders, the test files no scenario
      claims, one group per folder;
      and the runtime group. Stale and untested stories group as tests
      groups groups stories: stories one file cites join one group of up
      to 8, split in catalog order, and the rest are batched by 8 in
      catalog order. It refuses unless testing.enabled is on.
  tests scenarios [--json]
      The scenario list as it stands, each story's test stamp first.
  tests convert-state [--clear <story slug> ...] [--dry-run]
      Convert the scenario list from the earlier form, one top-level
      stamp for the whole list, to the form this tool reads. It reads
      no testing config. The first line it prints says the form:
      none: where the file does not exist, current: where it holds no
      top-level stamp, and earlier: where it does; only the earlier
      form converts. Each story with recorded scenarios gets as its
      test stamp the git blob id of .ok-planner/design/stories/<slug>.md
      at the stamp's commit, and none where that commit is not in the
      repository, the file is absent at it, or --clear names the story.
      The conversion drops the top-level stamp, writes ranking,
      acceptances, and reaches empty, and keeps kept and every other
      field. --dry-run prints the converted file, exactly as the write
      would leave it, on standard output, its other lines on standard
      error, and writes nothing; otherwise it replaces the file
      atomically.
  tests declare [--enabled on|frozen|off] [--run <command>] [--silence <seconds>]
                [--named yes|no] [--leftovers <command>] [--slowest <n>]
                [--runners [<command> ...]] [--setup <command> | none]
                [--setup-silence <seconds>]
                [--schedule <recommended | groups-side-by-side | groups-one-at-a-time>
                 --schedule-said <the owner's words>]
                [--reaches <files | folders | none> --reaches-said <the owner's words>]
                [--offers [untested | stale | legacy | runtime ...]]
      Write the keys given into testing in .ok-planner/config.json, keep
      the others, and drop the earlier testing.timeout. /ok-setup is the
      verb that runs it. enabled is on, frozen, or off; under off,
      groups, plan (but plan --show and plan --questions), reached,
      slowest, reports, settled, tasks --stage speed, and run --sprint
      refuse, and every other verb, run without --sprint among them,
      still runs. offers lists the standing offers /plan-sprint
      makes, each kind at most once, which enabled on needs. run is the
      suite command, holding {tests} and {results}; silence, the whole seconds
      with no result after which a run stops; named, whether the suite
      command runs a test by its name inside its file; leftovers, a
      command that prints one JSON object counting each kind of resource
      a test may leave behind, {} where it counts none; slowest, how
      many of the slowest tests each run lists; runners, the project's
      own test-running commands, which review checks refuses as a check,
      none where it names no runner of its own; setup, a command each
      run runs before the suite command to get the test environment
      ready, safe to run again and fast where the environment is ready,
      or none, written null, where the project needs no setup;
      setup_silence, the whole seconds with no output after which setup
      stops, which a declared setup needs; and schedule, {"form",
      "said"}, the schedule the suite command runs and the owner's words
      for it. The forms are recommended, where two tests may run at the
      same time unless one changes a resource the other changes or
      reads; groups-side-by-side, where held groups run at the same time
      as each other after the isolated tests; and groups-one-at-a-time,
      where held groups run one after another after the isolated tests.
      --schedule and --schedule-said go together. While said is null,
      as true-up writes it, the tool ranks by the form and checks no
      pair of tests against it. reaches, {"form", "said"}, records what
      the suite command writes as each test's reaches and the owner's
      words for it: files, where each line names the product files the
      test ran; folders, where it names the folders of the services the
      test reached; or none, where it writes no reaches. --reaches and
      --reaches-said go together. While said is null, the tool uses the
      form the key names. --timeout is refused: a run stops after
      testing.silence seconds with no result or output instead.
  tests enabled
      Print testing.enabled: on, frozen, or off. It needs no other
      testing key, and refuses where testing.enabled is missing or holds
      any other value.
  tests budget --from-run <run>
      Write the test stack's budget into the scenario list from the
      latest done budget task of the run /ok-setup opened, with its
      limits, refusing as tests plan refuses a budget that does not
      hold, and print it.
  tests run [--sprint <path> [--failing | --changed]] [--only <scenario> ...] [--from <file>] [--json]
      Run the tests in one run of testing.run, the project's suite
      command. {tests} becomes the path of a JSON file, {"workers": <n>,
      "tests": [{"path", "name", "resources": [...], "reads": [...]}]},
      the run using at most those workers, and {results} the
      path of a stream the command writes one line to per test as each
      finishes: {"path", "name", "passed": true or false, "seconds":
      <number>, "output": "<text>", "reaches": [<path>, ...]}, reaches
      optional: the paths inside the project the test reached, each
      ending in / a folder that covers every file under it. A reaches
      that is not a list of non-empty paths inside the project is
      dropped with a note, and the result stands. The command writes
      its lines to that path and never replaces the file. Several workers may write it at
      once, so each line, newline included, stays within %d bytes, the
      least PIPE_BUF POSIX allows: cap output to fit. A test's first
      line stands; a later line, or one that is not JSON or lacks path,
      passed, or seconds, is unmatched; a test with no line fails where
      the round ran to its end, and is not run where the run stopped or
      the round did not close, which no failure reader counts as its
      own failure: tests failures lists a stop that left a test with no
      result as one stopped--silence entry. The run records each line's arrival, its seconds since the suite
      command started, and takes the test's start as its arrival less
      its seconds.
      Where testing.setup is a command, the run runs it first, with no
      clock but testing.setup_silence seconds with no output, and starts
      the suite command only after setup exits 0; a failed setup fails
      every test with a tail that opens setup failed. The run stops
      after testing.silence seconds with no result line and no output,
      or, while a test that records a silence of its own has no result,
      after the longest such silence. Without
      --sprint, the tests the stamp recorded; with --sprint, the tests
      the sprint plans, each test with the shared resources it declares.
      Every run uses the budget's workers, the plan's with --sprint and
      the stamp's without, one where it records none. Every run that
      names --sprint records a round in the sprint's -results.jsonl, of
      kind whole where it runs the whole suite and of kind rerun
      otherwise: it prints each result as it lands, and appends the whole
      round, its header, its results, and its close, once at its end,
      under a short lock, inside which the round takes its number. The
      header records the git blob of each test file in the plan's
      folders and of each support file its tests name, and each task
      closed by then in the sprint's run and, as <ledger>#<task>, in its
      certification ledger. A run
      that fails partway still appends the results it has, its close
      recording the error, then fails. Around a recorded round,
      testing.leftovers runs before and after, and the round records
      both counts and each kind whose count grew, which fails the round.
      A whole-suite round refuses at once while another whole-suite
      round of the same sprint is under way, naming that run and the
      results file. Before testing.setup and the suite command start, a
      run locks each shared resource its tests declare, in slug order,
      until the run ends: an exclusive lock for a resource a test
      changes, a shared lock for one its tests only read. A run of
      isolated tests takes no lock. A run that must wait for a lock
      another run holds prints one line naming that run and the
      resource, then waits for it. Each live test's standing result is
      its latest result from the latest closed whole-suite round onward,
      that round included, with the blobs its round's header recorded.
      --failing reruns each live test whose standing result failed and
      no hold settles, as tests failures says, never a not run test; it refuses
      where no round is recorded or no such test stands, which sends you
      to the whole suite as the gate. --changed reruns each live test
      whose test file or support files differ from the blobs of its
      standing result, or that has no standing result or one not run,
      and refuses where none does, naming the whole suite. --only,
      --from, --failing, and --changed each choose the tests, so a run
      takes at most one; --failing and --changed need --sprint.
      --only prints the testing.leftovers counts around its run,
      recorded or not; --from runs the tests a JSON list of {path,
      name, scenario} names, takes no --sprint, and records no round.
      It prints each result, then the
      testing.slowest slowest tests by what each costs the run's wall
      time under testing.schedule's form: the tool takes a lower bound
      on the round's wall time, and a test's cost is how much the bound
      drops when its seconds drop to zero. A group is the tests that
      share a changed resource, and an isolated test changes none. Under
      recommended, the bound is the larger of the longest group's total
      seconds and all tests' seconds over the workers; under
      groups-side-by-side, the isolated tests' seconds over the workers
      plus the larger of the longest group's total and the held tests'
      seconds over the workers; under groups-one-at-a-time, the isolated
      tests' seconds over the workers plus every group's seconds. Every
      slowest list this tool prints or reads is ranked this way, a
      round's from the workers and resources its header records, or,
      for a round an earlier release recorded without them, the plan's
      budget workers and the resources each live test declares.
  tests reached --sprint <path> --files <path> ...
      The scenario ids a fix that changed the files given reruns, one
      per line. The map of each test's reaches is the scenario list's,
      overlaid by the sprint's rounds in order: each run of a test
      replaces its reaches, and a run that carries none leaves the test
      with no record; a not run result changes nothing. Where
      testing.reaches names files or folders, it prints each live test
      whose reaches names a file given or a folder above one, and each
      live test with no reaches; where no live test's reaches names a
      file given, every live test. Where testing.reaches is none, it
      prints none and says to rerun only the tests the fix was for.
      Run them with tests run --sprint <path> --only.
  tests groups --sprint <path> --from-run <planning run> [--size <n>]
               [--replan <story slug> ...] [--take <offer key> ...]
               [--new <story slug> ...] [--widen [<story slug> ...]]
               [--frozen] [--json]
      Write the planning groups beside the sprint, -groups.json in
      place of .md, and print each group's key, its stories with the
      reasons each is touched, and its brief, and the inputs every
      planner's brief carries. --replan touches each tested story whose
      behavior the sprint changes, with the reason behavior. --take
      takes each offer group `tests offers` prints by its key: a stale
      or untested group touches each of its stories with the reason
      stale or untested, a legacy group puts its folder's test files in
      the sort, and the runtime group runs the runtime planner. --new
      touches each story the sprint's deltas add, with the reason new,
      and refuses a slug no ### New story: <slug> delta of the sprint
      names. --widen touches each story it names, with the reason
      widen; with no slug, each story whose earlier calls, as `tests
      calls` lists them, left a path out. --frozen marks every group's
      brief frozen, so its planner plans no new scenario; it is given
      exactly where testing.enabled is frozen, and takes no --take,
      --new, or --widen. It lists the test files to sort, those of the
      taken legacy folders, and as kept, left as they stand, every other
      test file no scenario claims. It prints nothing to plan where no
      story is touched, no legacy folder or runtime group is taken, and
      the owner answered no program question no-guard: the plan records
      each no-guard answer in the scenario list, so a sprint with one
      assembles a plan. Stories one file cites join one
      group of up to --size stories (8 where absent), split in catalog
      order; the other touched stories are batched by that size in
      catalog order. It refuses a run with a program question the owner
      has not answered, a --replan or --widen slug with no story file,
      and an offer key `tests offers` does not print.
  tests calls <story slug> ... [--json]
      For each story, the archived sprint whose plan planned it last,
      or an earlier release's archived test sprint, newest first by the
      closed: stamps, and that plan's calls
      for the story that left a path out: each call of kind untested,
      or every call of the story in a plan whose calls carry no kind.
      A plan that records no planned stories planned every story. A
      call belongs to a story by its story field, else by a site or
      key that is the story's slug or starts with <slug>--.
  tests plan --sprint <path> --from-run <planning run> [--frozen]
      Assemble the sprint's plan from the planners' items and the
      groups `tests groups` wrote, check it, and write it beside the
      sprint. It takes each story's
      items from the latest done story task that records a stories item
      for it, keeps each recorded scenario of a story no group lists,
      and of a story whose every reason is widen each recorded
      scenario its planner names nowhere, and refuses a touched story
      no done story task records, and a run with no groups. It refuses
      each new or rewritten scenario with no entry, kind, or setup, or
      with an entry the owner left unguarded. --frozen is given exactly
      where testing.enabled is frozen, and refuses each new scenario.
      It refuses a legacy entry whose path lies outside the legacy
      folders the groups took, and a test file to sort that no
      scenario claims and the sort names nowhere, and records as kept
      every test file the groups keep that no planned scenario claims:
      each unclaimed test file outside the taken folders. It takes the
      budget and its limits from the latest done budget task, and
      refuses one that records no budget item or more than one, workers
      that is not a whole number of 1 or more, a budget that names no
      stack or no sizing, and a limit handled other than by raise,
      split, or lease. Where no budget task closed done, the plan
      carries the budget the scenario list records, its preflight mark
      included, and checks none. It refuses a scenario's resources or
      reads that is not a list of slugs. It takes the
      time controls from the controls items, one per id, and refuses
      two that record one id with different fields, a new or rewritten
      scenario with no waits or one that is none of event: <what the
      product emits>, control: <a time control>, and clock: <the clock
      the test moves>, a new or rewritten scenario's waits that names a
      control--<slug> no item records, and a time control whose form is not trigger, clock, or
      setting, that names no site or no files, that names no gate where
      it is a trigger or a clock or one where it is a setting, or that
      no scenario waits on; each becomes a work item of the sprint, which
      a code stage builds. It keeps each fixture a kept test uses as
      that test's support and deletes each one no kept test uses, and
      refuses a deletion or port of a file a kept scenario names as
      support, a support entry that names no test that reads it, a
      port of rule inferred that states no inferred rule, and inferred
      text beside any other sort or rule.
      Where testing.named is false, an adopted scenario's test is its
      whole file.
  tests plan --sprint <path> --from-run <planning run> --questions
      The questions that no answer settles yet, from the planner tasks
      whose items the assembly takes, as JSON.
  tests plan --sprint <path> --show [--json]
      Print the plan: a summary first (scenarios per story by action,
      the untested and departure calls whole, the other calls by kind,
      each inferred rule a port enforces with its test and check, and
      the planned tests by kind and setup), then the counts and
      the listing, each shared resource with its tests, the budget, and
      each time control the plan builds. --json prints the plan file as
      it stands.
  tests tasks --sprint <path> --stage <write | sweep | verify | speed> [--run <run file>]
              [--after <task> ...] [--from state | --round <n>]
      File the sprint's test tasks for one stage into a task run: the
      run file --run names, which must exist, or else the sprint's
      -run.jsonl. --after, which only the write stage takes, names the
      build tasks of the code stages the test stages follow: every task
      the write stage files new, the preflight included, waits on each
      of them, and the stage refuses, filing nothing, where one names no
      task of the run or closed other than done. On a run other than the
      sprint's -run.jsonl, such as the certification ledger, the write
      stage files only what that run's own items ask for: its sent-back
      tests, its blocked test tasks, and its open support items; it
      files no preflight, no planned test, and no legacy task. Where the
      plan's budget names a test stack, the write stage first
      files the preflight task alone, refiles it where it closed short
      of done, refuses where it closed blocked twice, and files the rest
      only once it closed done. It files no preflight where the plan's
      budget carries a preflight mark and equals the budget the
      scenario list records: a budget a budget task produced carries
      no mark, so it gets a preflight. The write stage files a support task per
      support file the written scenarios name, and per support file an
      open support item names, then the test tasks, which wait on them,
      then the legacy tasks. A support item, in the support pool, names
      a support file as its path, its scenario, and as its change the
      support a test needs: missing, a missing helper; changed, a
      changed helper; fixture; or seed, a seed entry. It stands open
      until the support task marks it made. The stage refiles each test
      task that closed blocked having filed a support item after the
      support task that makes it, and refiles the test task of each
      scenario whose latest tests item a verifier or a speed reviewer
      sent back, with the reason, after any task that holds its files.
      That rewrite's files hold its test file and its scenario's
      support files, the plan's and the tests item's, so it may change
      them, and two rewrites that share a file run one after the other.
      The stage refuses, and files nothing, where a support item
      records another change, where an open support item's support task
      closed done, where a scenario's last two test tasks closed blocked
      and neither filed a support item, and where a task it would file
      or refile waits on a task that closed other than done, naming that
      task and its outcome. The verify
      stage files one verify task per scenario whose latest tests item
      records a test and stands open, with no task before it, its brief
      naming the test's seconds in the latest round its test task ran it
      in, the budget's workers, the shared resources the test declares
      it changes and those it declares it reads, each declaration and
      plan text the item changes, before and after, and each resource it
      adds, and one per fixes or promises item that stands open, each
      after the last. On a run other than the sprint's -run.jsonl, where
      verify names the fix verifier, the verify stage files its tasks
      with the test-verify prompt. The speed stage takes exactly one source, as tests
      slowest does: --from state, the ranking the scenario list carries,
      in the build stages, or --round <n>, the failed whole-suite round
      sprint certification names. It files one batch: the
      testing.slowest unsettled tests tests slowest prints for that
      source, one speed task each, keyed speed:<scenario>:<the git blob
      of its test file>, save a test whose latest speed verdict sent it
      back, which settles once its rewrite is accepted. Under --from
      state it refuses where a tests item stands sent back or awaits its
      verifier; under --round <n> it refuses unless round n is a closed
      whole-suite round that lists a failure. It refiles a speed task
      that closed short of done or with no verdict, or whose test is
      unsettled again, and refuses where two closed short of done or
      with no verdict. --from and --round go with --stage speed alone.
  tests slowest --sprint <path> (--from state | --round <n>) [--json]
      Print the testing.slowest slowest tests whose speed is unsettled,
      each with its rank, the git blob of its test file, the digest of
      its support files, its median seconds and the count of runs the
      median spans, and its cost, and write nothing. A test's median is
      the median of its passing results in the closed whole-suite rounds
      whose header records its test file at the blob it has now, and of
      the seconds the scenario list's ranking carries for it at that
      blob. --from state ranks every live test by the carried seconds
      alone, with the budget's workers and the resources each live test
      declares; --round <n> ranks the tests round n ran, a closed
      whole-suite round, by the rounds up to n, with the workers and
      resources its header records. Each cost is what the test's median
      costs the wall time under testing.schedule's form, as tests run
      ranks. A test is settled where its latest speed verdict accepted
      it at the blob its test file has now, with the support digest it
      has now or a median no higher than the seconds accepted; where its
      latest verdict sent it back and its scenario's latest tests item,
      recorded after that verdict, stands accepted; or where its latest
      verdict found it product-slow and no accepted fixes item names
      that verdict with perf. A test with no verdict in the run is
      settled where the acceptance the scenario list carries for it
      holds by the same rule. It refuses neither source and both, and a
      round that is no closed whole-suite round.
  tests ports --sprint <path>
      Add each project check the latest legacy task of each file
      recorded to the review loop's checks. It writes nothing, and
      refuses, where checks is not a list of commands or holds one that
      starts a test runner, and where a recorded command starts a test
      runner, as review checks finds one, naming each such ports item,
      the legacy task that recorded it, and the runner, and saying to
      refile that task with the refusal.
  tests snapshot --sprint <path> --key <fix key> [--after] <file> ...
      Keep each file as it stands before a fixer first edits it, or,
      with --after, every file the before snapshot holds as the fixer
      leaves it when it closes.
  tests diff --sprint <path> --key <fix key>
      The change one fixer made, from its two snapshots.
  tests stray --sprint <path> [--json]
      The test files, and the support files the stamp or the plan
      records, that no planned test claims as its own or as support,
      save each test file the plan or the stamp records as kept.
  tests failures --sprint <path> [--json]
      Each live test whose standing result failed, since the latest
      closed whole-suite round, and, from each round since then,
      leftovers--<kind> for each kind of leftover whose count grew in
      it, stopped--silence where it stopped after testing.silence and
      left a test with no result, naming those tests, and
      schedule--<test>--<test> for each pair of tests that ran at the
      same time where testing.schedule's form
      forbids it: under recommended, one changes a resource the other
      changes or reads; under groups-side-by-side, a held test beside an
      isolated one, or two tests of one group; under
      groups-one-at-a-time, a held test beside any other. Two tests ran
      at the same time where each started before the other's arrival.
      It checks no pair while testing.schedule.said is null, or for a
      round that records no arrival times. It also lists
      perf:<scenario>:<speed item> for each scenario's latest
      product-slow speed verdict, in the sprint's run or its
      certification ledger, that no accepted fixes item of that run
      names with perf=<speed item> and no verified defect of the ledger
      names as its test, with or without a round. It leaves out each
      failure a hold settles: an owner issue the plan records, a held
      promise of the sprint's run, a promised defect of the
      certification ledger whose test field names it, and an open issue
      the scenario list records on the scenario.
  tests reports --sprint <path>
      Add one report to the selected run, which must be a certification
      ledger of the sprint, under key gate and area sprint-suite, for
      each failure of the latest closed whole-suite round (entry T1),
      its stopped--silence entry where it stopped and left a test with
      no result (T1), each leftover count that grew in it (T2), each
      perf: entry tests
      failures lists (T3), and each schedule-- entry of the round (T4),
      each with entry, test, the failure's id, round, the round's
      number, files, the reaches of its tests, and site. A T1 report
      also carries rerun, passed or failed, from the first rerun round
      after it that ran that test alone. It adds no report for a
      failure a hold settles, as tests failures says, nor one a report
      of the run already names, by test and round, or by test alone for
      perf:. It prints JSON: the reports it added, the failures already
      reported, and the failures held.
  tests settled --sprint <path> --ledger <ledger>
      Check the sprint's suite for certification and write nothing. The
      ledger must be the one this tool reads for the sprint. It holds
      where a closed whole-suite round is recorded, no task that changes
      files, of role preflight, support, test, legacy, sweep, build,
      fix, or restore, closed after it in the sprint's run or the
      ledger with a path outside the test paths staged, and every live
      test has a result since that round; where no failure tests
      failures lists stands; and where each speed send-back, the latest
      verdict on its test, is settled by a tests item accepted after it
      or its test file stands at the blob the verdict recorded, its last
      passing version. It prints SETTLED, or NOT SETTLED and each
      failure, and exits 2 where it does not hold.
  tests stamp --sprint <archived path> --commit <sha>
      After the owner commits the sprint's work, for a sprint archived
      under .ok-planner/history/sprints/: write the scenario list the
      sprint's run and its certification ledger leave, and write no
      other file. A sprint with no -plan.json reads as a plan of no
      tests, so every scenario, story stamp, unguarded program, budget,
      and kept file stands as recorded, and the stamp writes the
      ranking, the speed acceptances, and the reaches its rounds
      leave. It records each scenario whose
      test the sprint wrote or kept, with its test, support files,
      resources, reads, silence, waits, kind, and setup, and each gap;
      a planned new scenario whose test was never written gets no
      record, and a planned rewrite or retirement the sprint never
      made keeps its earlier record. Each scenario the plan does not
      name stands as recorded. A scenario failing in its standing
      result records the open issue it waits on: an owner issue, a
      held promise, or the issue on a promised ledger defect. Each
      story the plan planned whose every planned scenario has its
      test written, kept, or retired, or is a gap, gets as its test
      stamp the git blob id of .ok-planner/design/stories/<slug>.md at
      <sha>; every other story the plan planned loses its stamp. It
      writes the ranking, each test's median over the passing
      whole-suite runs at its test file's blob, the ranking's carried
      values included, charged by testing.schedule, and adds the sprint
      to ranked_sprints, the sprints whose rounds the ranking holds; a
      stamp of a sprint ranked_sprints already names ranks from the
      carried values alone, so a second stamp counts no round twice;
      each test's speed
      acceptance, the latest accepted verdict, or the carried one
      where no verdict was given, while its test file stands at the
      accepted blob; and each test's reaches, the latest run's list
      over the carried map. It also writes each inferred rule the run
      ported, {rule, check, command, test, sprint}, beside the ones
      earlier stamps recorded, the test files the plan kept, and the
      plan's budget, its preflight mark set to the id of the sprint's
      latest preflight task where it closed done, else kept as the
      plan carries it. It refuses a scenario list still in the earlier
      form, with one top-level stamp: convert-state converts it.
  tests rules [--json]
      Each inferred rule a stamp recorded that no take settled and whose
      command still stands in the review loop's checks. It needs no
      testing config, and prints none where no stamp recorded one.
  tests rules take <check> --as practice:<slug> | retired
      Settle the inferred rule of one check: as the practice
      .ok-planner/practices/<slug>.md, which must exist, or retired. It
      refuses a check no stamp recorded, and one already settled.

The scenario list lives in .ok-planner/test-state.json, which only
this tool writes: each story's test stamp under stamps, the git blob
id of the story's file as a plan last planned it, each scenario with
its test, its support files, what its test waits on, its kind and
setup, the shared resources its test declares it changes, and those it
declares it reads and does not change, the plan's budget
with its preflight mark, the id of the preflight task that last passed
against it, each program the owner chose to leave unguarded,
each inferred rule a port carries, with how it was settled, the
ranking, each test's latest seconds at the git blob of its test file,
at most testing.slowest of them, with its median and cost, and each
test's speed acceptance, {blob, support, seconds}, and each test's
reaches, the product files and folders its latest run reached.
A scenario that records no resources declares none. A scenario that
records resources and no reads counts as reading every shared resource,
each one any live test declares it changes or reads, and one that
records neither is isolated. A plan or a stamp with no budget runs one
worker. A tests item's resources and reads, where it records them,
replace the plan's, additions included, and its reach, entry, waits,
kind, and setup replace the plan's. A tests item never changes a
scenario's text, its outcome.

A sprint with no -plan.json, such as one approved before sprints planned
their tests, plans no tests: run, failures, stray, tasks, and settled
read it as a plan of no scenarios over the project's folders, with the
budget and the kept files the scenario list records; plan --show,
run --changed, and stamp refuse it. A sprint's certification ledger is
the selected run where it is a /converge ledger whose sprint-review
prompt certifies the sprint, or else the ledger the completion report's
# Sprint certification section names; its tests items join the sprint
run's, the later per scenario.

Exit 0: done. Exit 1: usage, input, or read error. Exit 2: tests settled
finds the suite unsettled.""" % RESULT_LINE_BYTES

